{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RtewYC/translated_rust/Cargo.toml; printf '\\\\n--- VERIFICATION tail ---\\\\n'; tail -100 /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RtewYC/translated_rust/VERIFICATION.md; printf '\\\\n--- ERRORS tail ---\\\\n'; tail -100 /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-RtewYC/translated_rust/ERRORS.md\"","aggregated_output":"[package]\nname = \"sphincs_plus\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2021\"\ndescription = \"Rust translation of the SPHINCS+ reference implementation (build-time configurable)\"\n\n[lib]\nname = \"sphincs_plus\"\ncrate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"rlib\"]\npath = \"src/lib.rs\"\n\n[[bin]]\nname = \"driver\"\npath = \"src/main.rs\"\n\n[features]\ndefault = [\"blake\", \"simple\", \"128f\"]\n\n# ---- HASH_BACKEND ----\nharaka = []\nsha2 = []\nshake = []\n# alias accepted by the CMake docstring (\"shake256\")\nshake256 = [\"shake\"]\nblake = []\n\n# ---- THASH ----\nrobust = []\nsimple = []\n\n# ---- SECPAR ----\n\"128s\" = []\n\"128f\" = []\n\"192s\" = []\n\"192f\" = []\n\"256s\" = []\n\"256f\" = []\n\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n[profile.release]\nopt-level = 3\n\n[workspace]\n\n--- VERIFICATION tail ---\n3. **`gen_message_random` over-writes `R`** for the blake backend\n   (`blakeX_final` writes 32 or 64 bytes, not `SPX_N`).\n4. **`prf_addr` (blake) copies `sk_seed` into a buffer it never hashes**, so the\n   secret seed does not influence `prf_addr` for that backend.\n5. `randombytes(x, 0)` still advances `DRBG_ctx` (runs\n   `AES256_CTR_DRBG_Update` and `reseed_counter++`).\n6. `crypto_sign_open` zeroes the **full** `smlen` bytes of `m` on failure, not\n   `smlen - SPX_BYTES`.\n7. `seedexpander` rejects `xlen == ctx->length_remaining` (the check is `>=`).\n8. Every `set_*_addr` setter silently truncates its `uint32_t` argument to 8 bits\n   (except `set_keypair_addr` / `set_tree_index`, which write 4 big-endian bytes),\n   so out-of-range `SPX_ADDR_TYPE_*` enum values are accepted and wrapped.\n\n## Reproducing everything from scratch\n\n```sh\ncd translated_rust\n\n# 1. every feature combination compiles\nfor hb in haraka sha2 shake blake; do for th in robust simple; do\n  for sp in 128s 128f 192s 192f 256s 256f; do\n    cargo check --no-default-features --features \"$hb,$th,$sp\" --all-targets || echo \"FAIL $hb-$th-$sp\"\ndone; done; done\n\n# 2. symbol parity for all 48 (builds C + Rust)\n./scripts/sweep_build.sh          # -> target/sweep_build.txt\n\n# 3. the full differential suite for all 48\n./scripts/sweep_test.sh           # -> target/sweep_test.txt\n\n# 4. independent end-to-end KAT digests for all 48\nfor hb in haraka sha2 shake blake; do for th in robust simple; do\n  for sp in 128s 128f 192s 192f 256s 256f; do\n    ./scripts/driver_digests.sh $hb $th $sp\ndone; done; done\n```\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] `SYMBOLS.md`: `nm -D` shows **0** missing symbols and **0** undefined\n      non-libc symbols in the Rust `.so`, for all 48 configurations.\n- [x] Phase B: **every** row in `CONFIGS.md` (B01–B50 + B24b) passes across\n      randomized inputs (fixed-seed SplitMix64), in all 48 configurations.\n- [x] Phase C: **every** row in `ERRORS.md` (E01–E39, minus the two documented\n      non-triggerable ones) has a passing error-path differential test that\n      compares the exact error code, in all 48 configurations.\n- [x] All of the above hold under **every** one of the 48 feature combinations.\n\n## Harness pitfall that had to be fixed (and is now guarded)\n\n`cargo test` builds the library target only as an **rlib** for the integration\ntests — it does **not** refresh the `cdylib` that the tests `dlopen`.  Verified\ndirectly: `touch src/utils.rs && cargo test` leaves\n`target/.../release/libsphincs_plus.so` untouched.  A naive harness would\ntherefore keep testing the *previous* build of the Rust code and pass no matter\nwhat was changed.\n\nTwo mitigations are in place:\n\n1. `tests/common/mod.rs::assert_not_stale` compares the `.so`'s mtime against the\n   newest `src/**/*.rs` and **panics** if the `.so` is older, printing the exact\n   `cargo build` command to run.  (Bypass with `SPX_ALLOW_STALE_SO=1` only if you\n   know why.)\n2. `scripts/sweep_test.sh` runs `cargo build --release` before\n   `cargo test --release` for every configuration.\n\n## Mutation testing — proof the suite is actually sensitive\n\nSeven deliberate single-token bugs were injected into the Rust (one at a time,\neach reverted afterwards; the source tree was checksum-verified back to its\noriginal state).  Every one was caught, including both error-path mutations:\n\n| # | mutation | caught by |\n|---|---|---|\n| M1 | `SPX_bytes_to_ull`: shift amount `+ 8` | `b03_bytes_to_ull` |\n| M2 | `SPX_set_type`: write at `SPX_OFFSET_TYPE + 1` | `b04_address_all`, `b33_treehash`, `b34_fors_gen_leafx1` |\n| M3 | AES S-box entry `[4]` `0xf2 → 0xf3` | `b47_aes256_ecb`, `b46_drbg`, `b48_drbg_update`, `b50_kat_transcript`, … |\n| M4 | `base_w`: mask `W-1 → W-2` | `b27_chain_lengths` |\n| M5 | `blake256_init`: IV `h[7]` `0x5BE0CD19 → 0x5BE0CD18` | `b06_blake256`, `b18_prf_addr`, `b19_thash_1`, … |\n| M6 | `crypto_sign_open`: zero `smlen - SPX_BYTES` instead of `smlen` | `e04_open_verify_fails` |\n| M7 | `seedexpander`: `xlen >= length_remaining` → `>` | `e08_seedexpander_bad_req_len` |\n| M8 | `seedexpander`: counter loop `i >= 12` → `i >= 13` | `b49b_seedexpander_ctr_carry_chain` (and **only** that test) |\n| M9 | `randombytes`: `V` carry loop `j >= 0` → `j >= 1` | `b46b_randombytes_v_carry_chain` (and **only** that test) |\n\nM1 was initially reported as *missed* — which is exactly how the stale-cdylib\npitfall above was discovered.  After adding the staleness guard and the explicit\n`cargo build`, all of them are caught.\n\nM8/M9 were added after a second mechanical pass over **every** `if`/`for`/`while`\nin `c_src/app/src/*.c` revealed the only two branches with no test:\nthe 128-bit `DRBG_ctx.V` carry chain in `randombytes` (`rng.c:160-167`) and the\n`ctr[15..12]` carry chain in `seedexpander` (`rng.c:91-98`).  Reaching them\nthrough the public API would need 2^8…2^128 calls, so rows **B46b** and **B49b**\nwrite the state directly through the exported `DRBG_ctx` symbol / a caller-owned\n`AES_XOF_struct`.  M8/M9 confirm those two rows are the only thing covering\nthose loops — i.e. the gap was real and is now closed.\n\nThe source tree was checksum-verified (`find src -name '*.rs' | sort | xargs\nmd5sum | md5sum` → `4e8a284cb0162955077148aba4466fa3`) identical before and\nafter all mutation experiments.\n\n--- ERRORS tail ---\n\n## 7. Notable C behaviours discovered while writing these tests\n\nThese are **not** bugs in the Rust translation — they are properties of the C\nground truth that the Rust reproduces byte for byte, and they had to be\nunderstood before the error-path assertions could be written correctly.  Each was\nconfirmed by calling the C shared library directly.\n\n### N1. `hash_blake.c` passes *byte* counts to a *bit*-oriented API\n\n`blake{256,512}_update(S, data, datalen)` takes `datalen` in **bits**\n(`blake256.c:399` — the only in-tree caller that converts — does `inlen*8`).\nBut `lib/blake/src/hash_blake.c` passes byte counts:\n\n```c\nblakeX_update(&S, R,  SPX_N);          /* 16 bits  = 2 bytes of R  */\nblakeX_update(&S, pk, SPX_PK_BYTES);   /* 32 bits  = 4 bytes of pk */\nblakeX_update(&S, m,  mlen);           /* mlen bits = mlen/8 bytes */\n```\n\nSo for `HASH_BACKEND=blake`, `SPX_hash_message` only absorbs the first\n`SPX_N/8` bytes of `R`, the first `SPX_PK_BYTES/8` bytes of `pk` and the first\n`mlen/8` bytes of the message.  `SPX_gen_message_random` has the same issue\n(`hash_blake.c:65-67`).\n\n*Consequence for the error tests:* corrupting a **later** message byte, or a\nlater byte of `R`, does **not** change `(digest, tree, leaf_idx)`, so\n`crypto_sign_verify` legitimately still returns `0`.  `e02_verify_root_mismatch`\nand `e04_open_verify_fails` therefore decide mechanically — by calling the C's\nown `SPX_hash_message` — whether a corruption is observable at all, require\n`-1` exactly when it is, require `0` when it is not, and assert a minimum number\nof genuinely-rejected cases so the test cannot become vacuous.\n\n### N2. `blake*_update` is not a general streaming API\n\n`blake256.c:300-328` only merges the pending buffer into the current block when\n`left && ((datalen >> 3) & 0x3F) >= fill`.  Two consequences, both verified\nagainst the C library:\n\n* `blake256_update(S, p, 0)` falls into `else S->buflen = 0;` and **discards**\n  everything buffered so far;\n* feeding 1 byte and then 64 bytes compresses the 64 bytes and silently drops\n  the buffered byte.\n\nSo splitting a message across `blake*_update` calls changes the digest.\n`b06_blake256` / `b07_blake512` therefore compare **C against Rust** after every\n`init`/`update`/`final` call (which is the real requirement, and which covers\nexactly these quirky paths) instead of asserting incremental == one-shot.\n\n### N3. `gen_message_random` writes more than `SPX_N` bytes (blake only)\n\n`hash_blake.c:68` ends with `blakeX_final(&S, R)`, which writes\n`SPX_BLAKE{256,512}_OUTPUT_BYTES` = 32 or **64** bytes — not `SPX_N`.\n`sign.c:127` gets away with it because `R` points at the start of the\n`SPX_BYTES` signature buffer.  `b25_gen_message_random` uses an over-sized\noutput buffer, compares it in full, and additionally asserts that both sides\ntouch the *same number* of bytes.\n\n### N4. `SPX_prf_addr` (blake) copies `sk_seed` into a buffer it never hashes\n\n`hash_blake.c:40-49` builds `buf = pub_seed ‖ addr ‖ sk_seed` but then hashes\nonly `SPX_N + SPX_ADDR_BYTES` bytes, i.e. `pub_seed ‖ addr`.  The secret key\nseed does not influence `prf_addr` for the blake backend.  Reproduced exactly.\n\n## 8. Row → test mapping (every row has a passing differential test)\n\n| rows | test function (in `tests/phase_c_errors.rs`) |\n|---|---|\n| E01 | `e01_verify_wrong_siglen` |\n| E02 | `e02_verify_root_mismatch` |\n| E03 | `e03_open_smlen_too_small` |\n| E04 | `e04_open_verify_fails` |\n| E05, E06 | `e05_e06_seedexpander_init_maxlen` |\n| E07 | `e07_seedexpander_null_out` |\n| E08, E09, E10 | `e08_seedexpander_bad_req_len` |\n| E09, E10, E11 | `e09_e10_e11_seedexpander_success_paths` |\n| E12, E39 | `e12_e39_randombytes_lengths` |\n| E13 | not triggerable from the public API — documented only |\n| E14, E15 | `e14_e15_randombytes_init_personalization` |\n| E16, E17 | `e16_e17_drbg_update_null_provided_data` |\n| E18 | `e18_wots_gen_leafx1_null_sig` (also `b29_wots_gen_leafx1_pk`) |\n| E19, E20 | `e19_e20_no_auth_path_sentinel` |\n| E21 … E27 | `e21_set_type_out_of_range_enum` (covers all seven setters + `set_tree_addr`) |\n| E28, E29, E30 | `e28_e29_e30_ull_to_bytes_boundaries` |\n| E31, E32 | `e31_e32_bytes_to_ull_boundaries` |\n| E33 | `e33_thash_inblocks_zero` (also `b24_thash_0`) |\n| E34 | `e34_mgf1_outlen_zero` |\n| E35 | `e35_zero_length_message` |\n| E36 | `e36_open_empty_message` |\n| E37 | `e37_sha_inc_blocks_zero` |\n| E38 | `e38_squeeze_zero` |\n| C01 … C09 | compile-time `#error` guards — satisfied by all 48 configurations |\n| U01 … U05 | unbounded / crashing UB — documented, deliberately not executed |\n\n## 9. Result\n\n`scripts/sweep_test.sh` runs `tests/phase_c_errors.rs` (23 tests) for **all 48**\nfeature combinations.  `target/sweep_test.txt` records `PASS` for every one, so\n**every row above with a `[x]` has a passing error-path differential test in\nevery configuration.**\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
